1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:02,790
Welcome to the Surf Clock assembly guide.

2
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:05,740
The enclosure snaps together front to back

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:09,160
with zero screws and zero soldering.

4
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:11,780
Lay the outer frame face-down

5
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:13,490
so the rear cavity faces up.

6
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:16,710
Drop the dial face-down into the bezel.

7
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:20,880
Seat the motor mount plate on the posts,

8
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:25,357
and press 4 long snap-pins into the corners.

9
00:00:26,670 --> 00:00:30,230
Drop both 28BYJ-48 stepper motors

10
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:31,670
into their circular seats

11
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:34,030
so the shafts poke through the dial.

12
00:00:34,030 --> 00:00:38,370
Because of the 8mm shaft offset,

13
00:00:38,370 --> 00:00:40,370
orient the upper motor normally,

14
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:43,270
and flip the lower motor 180 degrees.

15
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:46,300
Push 4 short snap-pins through the ears

16
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:48,298
to lock both motors in place.

17
00:00:49,670 --> 00:00:53,770
Press the ESP32-S3 and mini breadboard

18
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:54,800
into the left pocket,

19
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:58,060
and seat both ULN2003 driver boards

20
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:00,460
into the right pocket.

21
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:03,600
Plug both 5-pin motor leads in,

22
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,720
tucking slack under the center arch.

23
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:11,610
Wire Upper IN1–IN4 to GPIO 4, 5, 6, 7.

24
00:01:11,610 --> 00:01:17,270
Wire Lower IN1–IN4 to GPIO 15, 16, 17, 18.

25
00:01:17,270 --> 00:01:20,780
Connect both driver 5V and GND pins

26
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:25,130
to the breadboard 5V VBUS and GND rails,

27
00:01:25,130 --> 00:01:27,682
and tuck all wires under the 6 clips.

28
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:31,900
Route the USB-C cable out the bottom slot,

29
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,870
place the back cover over the frame,

30
00:01:33,870 --> 00:01:36,480
and click in the final 4 long snap-pins.

31
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:37,890
Before plugging in power,

32
00:01:37,890 --> 00:01:39,710
flip the clock face-up.

33
00:01:39,710 --> 00:01:42,730
Each shaft has flat sides that key into

34
00:01:42,730 --> 00:01:45,100
the D-shaped bore inside the hands.

35
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:47,970
Press the long hand on at 12 o'clock

36
00:01:47,970 --> 00:01:50,070
pointing straight up at LONG REEF.

37
00:01:50,070 --> 00:01:53,020
Press the short hand on at 12 o'clock

38
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:55,482
pointing straight up on WORTH IT?.

39
00:01:56,670 --> 00:01:59,870
Plug in USB-C and your Surf Clock is live.

40
00:01:59,870 --> 00:02:02,940
Over Wi-Fi, the ESP32 fetches live

41
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:04,690
swell and wind forecasts,

42
00:02:04,690 --> 00:02:07,410
rotates both hands along the shortest arc,

43
00:02:07,410 --> 00:02:10,910
and powers down the coils to run silent.

44
00:02:10,910 --> 00:02:14,160
Built-in RTC and flash memory save

45
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,840
hand positions across sleep and power loss.

46
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:20,840
To re-zero anytime, point both hands

47
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:24,278
to 12 o'clock and hold BOOT on reset.

